tificates. However, exposure to high temperatures has wide-ranging effects on the human body, including the strain of keeping cool. This effort places pressure on the heart and kidneys . As a result, heat extremes can worsen health risks from chronic conditions and cause acute kidney injury. Researchers have linked heat to increased mortality from respiratory and cardiovascular diseases, as well as dementia and Alzheimerâ€™s .&nbsp; As a result, public health authorities and scientists cannot depend on death certificates for a full count of heat-related deaths. They instead estimate heat deaths using a number of different approaches, each with assumptions baked into their calculations.&nbsp; Dr Garyfallos Konstantinoudis , who researches methods for calculating excess mortality due to extreme events at the Grantham Institute for Climate Change and the Environment at Imperial College , tells Carbon Brief there is â€œno ground truthâ€ when it comes to tallying heat-related deaths:&nbsp; â€œWe donâ€™t know what the heat-related deaths are, so we rely on different models to describe the picture.â€ This makes the study of deaths from heatwaves similar to those from air pollution, he says: â€œThis sort of health-impact assessment has been done for years on studies related to deaths from air pollution, which have the same problem. Air pollution, until very recently , was not recorded on death certificates.&nbsp; â€œ[However], for air pollution, the [scientific] literature is much larger, so no one questions that air pollution is toxic and kills. This sort of messaging for heat is more recent.â€ There are, broadly speaking, two approaches to calculating deaths during a heatwave.&nbsp; The first involves counting the number of excess deaths relative to a period in the past.&nbsp; This method â€“ often referred to as an â€œexcess deathsâ€ approach â€“ looks at how many people died during a particular time period compared to a baseline period where there was no heatwave.&nbsp; To do this, public health authorities and researchers rely on official death figures reported by country authorities.&nbsp; The heat death tolls published in recent weeks by public health agencies in Belgium , France , Germany and the Netherlands relied on this approach.&nbsp; (For more, see: What are the pros and cons of the â€˜excess deathsâ€™ method? ) The second method uses long-term mortality data to understand the statistical relationship between temperature and mortality in a given place. The model that emerges can be used to infer the number of deaths from a heatwave in that place.&nbsp; In a rapid analysis published this week, researchers at Imperial College London , the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine (LSHTM) and the Met Office used this approach to estimate that the May and June heatwaves in the UK caused the deaths of 2,700 people. Dr Callahan â€“ working with Prof Andrew Dessler , director of the Texas Center for Extreme Weather at Texas A&M University â€“ used this method to estimate that more than 2,700 people had died in France over a 17-day period in June in an analysis for Carbon Brief .&nbsp; (For more: see: What are the pros and cons of the â€˜statistical modellingâ€™ method? ) The majority of the figures released in the wake of Europeâ€™s June heatwave have relied on these two methods. There is a third way to calculate heat deaths, which is to look at official counts of deaths attributed on death certificates to heatstroke. Callahan tells Carbon Brief that the â€œdeath-certificate codingâ€ appears to have fallen out of favour in Europe â€“ which he says is a â€œsmart moveâ€ given that it does not provide a â€œfull accountingâ€.&nbsp; Nevertheless, some public health authorities are still using this method. For example, in the wake of the heatwave in the US earlier this month, public health data showed 29 people in New Jersey and three people in New York had died from â€œheat-related illnessesâ€. Scientists tell Carbon Brief the excess deaths and statistical modelling approaches both have advantages and drawbacks. These are explored below. What do the latest figures show for Europeâ€™s May and June heatwaves? The table below shows the death numbers that have been reported by governments and researchers for Europeâ€™s May and June heatwaves, including the approach used to reach the figures. It suggests that multiple countries in Europe experienced more than 1,000 heat-related deaths during the late June heatwave, with authorities in Germany counting more than 5,000. Meanwhile, the EuroMoMo mortality monitoring system estimated there were more than 10,500 excess deaths across 27 countries during the June heatwave.&nbsp; Reported Source Country / region Dates Days Deaths Link Approach 28/06/2026 Public Health France â€‹ France 22-27 June 6 1,000 santepubliquefrance.fr Excess deaths 29/06/2026 World Health Organization Europe 21-28 June 8 1,300 x.com/DrTedros/status Excess deaths 01/07/2026 Carlos III Health Institute (MoMo) Spain 1-30 June 30 1,033 dw.com Excess deaths (all-cause and temperature-attributable) 02/07/2026 National Institute for Public Health and the Environment Netherlands 22-28 June 7 480 rivm.nl Excess deaths 03/07/2026 Public Health France â€‹ France 22-28 June 7 2,025 santepubliquefrance.fr Excess deaths 07/07/2026 Chris Callahan/Andrew Dessler France 12-29 June 18 2,766 carbonbrief.org Statistical modelling 08/07/2026 Chris Callahan Europe 15-28 June 14 13,975 zenodo.org Statistical modelling 08/07/2026 Sciensano Belgium 18 June - 1 July 14 1,747 brusselstimes.com Excess deaths 09/07/2026 Robert Koch Institute Germany 22-28 June 7 5,120 rki.de Statistical modelling 13/07/2026 Met Office/LSHTM/Imperial England and Wales 22-27 June 6 2,183 drive.google.com Statistical modelling 13/07/2026 Met Office/LSHTM/Imperial England and Wales 24-26 May 3 553 drive.google.com Statistical modelling 13/07/2026 EURO Mo/Mo 27 European countries 22-28 June 7 10,650 reuters.com Excess deaths 07/07/2025 National Institute for Public Health and the Environment Netherlands 22-28 June 7 577 archive.ph Excess deaths 14/07/2026 Germany Federal Statistical Office (Destatis) Germany 22-28 June 7 5,753 destatis.de Excess deaths In most instances, Carbon Brief has linked to the figures published by public health authorities, where numbers were first reported. In some instances, figures were released on dashboards or webpages that are updated weekly. In these cases, Carbon Brief has linked to media reports or archived web content.&nbsp; What are the pros and cons of the â€˜excess deathsâ€™ method? The excess deaths approach looks at how many more people died during a particular time period compared to a baseline period of the same length.&nbsp; For instance, on 14 July, Germanyâ€™s federal statistics agency, Destatis, published figures showing Germany saw 32% more deaths than the average in the week of 22-28 June, which was dominated by the heatwave. Specifically, the agency said that 23,932 deaths had been recorded that week, compared to an average of 18,179 in that calendar week across the years 2022-25. This suggests there were 5,753 excess deaths during the heatwave week. (This was a slight increase from preliminary Destatis figures released a week earlier, covered by Bloomberg .) The Netherlands similarly calculates excess deaths by comparing death figures against an average of deaths in a similar period during unspecified â€œprevious yearsâ€. Data published by the countryâ€™s National Institute for Public Health and the Environment (RIVM) shows that, during the week of 22-28 June, an estimated 3,626 people died in total in the northern European country.&nbsp; This is 577 more deaths than the 3,049 expected at that time of year, it said. (This is a slight revision upwards from the 480 excess deaths reported on 4 July by NL Times based on preliminary figures from NVIM.) Callahan says that the excess deaths approach has the benefit of being rapid and relatively uncomplicated: â€œIt is something that public health authorities can put out fairly quickly without having to run a fancy model and do coding like the academic scientists do. It is a short-term, high-impact, rapid estimate of mortality.â€&nbsp; The drawback to the approach is that it is impossible to decipher what percentage of these â€œall-mo...","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-20T00:28:20.261Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"q-a-europe-s-may-and-june-heatwave-deaths-and-how-they-were-counted","sourceUrl":"https://www.carbonbrief.org/qa-europes-may-and-june-heatwave-deaths-and-how-they-were-counted/","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"Fri, 17 Jul 2026 11:17:09 +0000","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-20T00:28:20.261Z"}],"warrant":{"status":"research-intake","supportClaimCount":4,"refutationClaimCount":0,"uncertainClaimCount":0,"sourceDomainCount":1,"sourceDomains":["carbonbrief.org"],"note":"Current-provider claims are source-bound research intake. Prior-corpus excerpts are context only and license no public factual claim."},"editorialRisks":[{"code":"demand-is-not-evidence","severity":"note","message":"The assignment and search terms select attention but license no factual claim."},{"code":"prior-research-is-context-only","severity":"note","message":"Recovered prior research may guide reporting but is not promoted into source claims by this service."},{"code":"prior-research-gaps","severity":"warning","message":"Corpus Management reported 5 explicit recovery gaps; do not infer that omitted or unmatched material is absent from the wider corpus."}],"sourceUrls":["https://www.carbonbrief.org/qa-europes-may-and-june-heatwave-deaths-and-how-they-were-counted/"],"candidateReferences":[{"id":"source-0d54e55ea431","type":"webpage","title":"Q&A: Europeâ€™s May and June heatwave deaths â€“ and how they were counted","URL":"https://www.carbonbrief.org/qa-europes-may-and-june-heatwave-deaths-and-how-they-were-counted/","container-title":"carbonbrief.org"}],"context":["[1] Q&A: Europeâ€™s May and June heatwave deaths â€“ and how they were counted (carbonbrief.org, 2026-07-17T11:17:09.000Z) - Recent weeks have seen a flurry of reports from public health authorities and scientists that estimate the deaths caused by Europeâ€™s record-breaking summer heatwaves .&nbsp; In France , the national public health agency reported 2,025 excess deaths over the week where the heatwave peaked in June.&nbsp; Authorities in Germany and Netherlands put the excess death toll during the same seven-day period at 5,753 and 533, respectively. An analysis from climate scientists in Carbon Brief found that France saw more than 2,700 heat-related deaths over 17 days in June. Separate research estimated there had been 2,700 heat-related deaths in the UKâ€™s May and June heatwaves â€“ 42% of which had been caused by human-caused climate change. There are a number of methods for how academics and governments tally deaths caused by extreme heat, each with their own advantages and drawbacks. Here, Carbon Brief looks at the different ways scientists and public health authorities have calculated the death toll of Europeâ€™s record-breaking summer heat.&nbsp; How established is the science of calculating heat deaths? What are the different approaches to counting heat deaths? What do the latest figures show for Europeâ€™s May and June heatwaves? What are the pros and cons of the â€˜excess deathsâ€™ method? What are the pros and cons of the â€˜statistical modellingâ€™ method? How established is the science of calculating heat deaths? Economists and epidemiologists have been studying the relationship between heat and mortality for nearly a century .&nbsp; A pioneering study published in 1923 by geographer Ellsworth Huntington and economist Margaret Justin that looked at mortality data for New York City over 1882-88 found that deaths increased rapidly as temperatures rose above 17C.&nbsp; As global temperatures have risen in response to human-caused carbon emissions, scientists have increasingly sought to understand how warming could impact mortality.&nbsp; The study of mortality caused by specific heatwave events dates back a few decades, with a 1995 heatwave in Chicago among the earliest events to be studied in detail. Over the past decade, a growing number of studies have gone a step further, by estimating the number of deaths caused by a specific heatwave event and then attributing a percentage or number of those deaths to human-caused climate change.&nbsp; Carbon Brief covered the first study of this type, which was published in Environmental Research Letters in 2016 and focused on a 2003 summer heatwave that caused tens of thousands of deaths across Europe. The study estimated that 506 of the 735 summer fatalities in Paris and 64 of the 315 in London were a result of human influence on the climate. More recently, a study in Climatic Change found that 27% of deaths in a 2018 heatwave in Zurich, Switzerland were linked to human-caused climate change and a paper in Science Advances estimated that 11-15% of deaths in a 2021 heatwave in British Columbia were attributable to global warming.&nbsp; Dr Christopher Callahan , assistant professor at the Oâ€™Neill School of Public and Environmental Affairs at Indiana University , tells Carbon Brief this type of â€œtwo-stepâ€ study has â€œreally explodedâ€ in recent years: â€œIt is really only in the last five to 10 years that we have seen this, partly because it does require interdisciplinary expertise. You need people who know how to run the epidemiological models and you need a climate analysis of the counterfactual [world] without climate change, which is its own effort.â€&nbsp; What are the different approaches to counting heat deaths? A central challenge in estimating deaths from a heatwave is that heat is rarely recorded as the primary cause of death on death certificates. However, exposure to high temperatures has wide-ranging effects on the human body, including the strain of keeping cool. This effort places pressure on the heart and kidneys . As a result, heat extremes can worsen health risks from chronic conditions and cause acute kidney injury. Researchers have linked heat to increased mortality from respiratory and cardiovascular diseases, as well as dementia and Alzheimerâ€™s .&nbsp; As a result, public health authorities and scientists cannot depend on death certificates for a full count of heat-related deaths. They instead estimate heat deaths using a number of different approaches, each with assumptions baked into their calculations.&nbsp; Dr Garyfallos Konstantinoudis , who researches methods for calculating excess mortality due to extreme events at the Grantham Institute for Climate Change and the Environment at Imperial College , tells Carbon Brief there is â€œno ground truthâ€ when it comes to tallying heat-related deaths:&nbsp; â€œWe donâ€™t know what the heat-related deaths are, so we rely on different models to describe the picture.â€ This makes the study of deaths from heatwaves similar to those from air pollution, he says: â€œThis sort of health-impact assessment has been done for years on studies related to deaths from air pollution, which have the same problem. Air pollution, until very recently , was not recorded on death certificates.&nbsp; â€œ[However], for air pollution, the [scientific] literature is much larger, so no one questions that air pollution is toxic and kills. This sort of messaging for heat is more recent.â€ There are, broadly speaking, two approaches to calculating deaths during a heatwave.&nbsp; The first involves counting the number of excess deaths relative to a period in the past.&nbsp; This method â€“ often referred to as an â€œexcess deathsâ€ approach â€“ looks at how many people died during a particular time period compared to a baseline period where there was no heatwave.&nbsp; To do this, public health authorities and researchers rely on official death figures reported by country authorities.&nbsp; The heat death tolls published in recent weeks by public health agencies in Belgium , France , Germany and the Netherlands relied on this approach.&nbsp; (For more, see: What are the pros and cons of the â€˜excess deathsâ€™ method? ) The second method uses long-term mortality data to understand the statistical relationship between temperature and mortality in a given place. The model that emerges can be used to infer the number of deaths from a heatwave in that place.&nbsp; In a rapid analysis published this week, researchers at Imperial College London , the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine (LSHTM) and the Met Office used this approach to estimate that the May and June heatwaves in the UK caused the deaths of 2,700 people. Dr Callahan â€“ working with Prof Andrew Dessler , director of the Texas Center for Extreme Weather at Texas A&M University â€“ used this method to estimate that more than 2,700 people had died in France over a 17-day period in June in an analysis for Carbon Brief .&nbsp; (For more: see: What are the pros and cons of the â€˜statistical modellingâ€™ method? ) The majority of the figures released in the wake of Europeâ€™s June heatwave have relied on these two methods. There is a third way to calculate heat deaths, which is to look at official counts of deaths attributed on death certificates to heatstroke. Callahan tells Carbon Brief that the â€œdeath-certificate codingâ€ appears to have fallen out of favour in Europe â€“ which he says is a â€œsmart moveâ€ given that it does not provide a â€œfull accountingâ€.&nbsp; Nevertheless, some public health authorities are still using this method. For example, in the wake of the heatwave in the US earlier this month, public health data showed 29 people in New Jersey and three people in New York had died from â€œheat-related illnessesâ€. Scientists tell Carbon Brief the excess deaths and statistical modelling approaches both have advantages and drawbacks. These are explored below. What do the latest figures show for Europeâ€™s May and June heatwaves? The table below shows the death numbers that have been reported by governments and researchers for Europeâ€™s May and June heatwaves, including the approach used to reach the figures. It suggests that multiple countries in Europe experienced more than 1,000 heat-related deaths during the late June heatwave, with authorities in Germany counting more than 5,000. Meanwhile, the EuroMoMo mortality monitoring system estimated there were more than 10,500 excess deaths across 27 countries during the June heatwave.&nbsp; Reported Source Country / region Dates Days Deaths Link Approach 28/06/2026 Public Health France â€‹ France 22-27 June 6 1,000 santepubliquefrance.fr Excess deaths 29/06/2026 World Health Organization Europe 21-28 June 8 1,300 x.com/DrTedros/status Excess deaths 01/07/2026 Carlos III Health Institute (MoMo) Spain 1-30 June 30 1,033 dw.com Excess deaths (all-cause and temperature-attributable) 02/07/2026 National Institute for Public Health and the Environment Netherlands 22-28 June 7 480 rivm.nl Excess deaths 03/07/2026 Public Health France â€‹ France 22-28 June 7 2,025 santepubliquefrance.fr Excess deaths 07/07/2026 Chris Callahan/Andrew Dessler France 12-29 June 18 2,766 carbonbrief.org Statistical modelling 08/07/2026 Chris Callahan Europe 15-28 June 14 13,975 zenodo.org Statistical modelling 08/07/2026 Sciensano Belgium 18 June - 1 July 14 1,747 brusselstimes.com Excess deaths 09/07/2026 Robert Koch Institute Germany 22-28 June 7 5,120 rki.de Statistical modelling 13/07/2026 Met Office/LSHTM/Imperial England and Wales 22-27 June 6 2,183 drive.google.com Statistical modelling 13/07/2026 Met Office/LSHTM/Imperial England and Wales 24-26 May 3 553 drive.google.com Statistical modelling 13/07/2026 EURO Mo/Mo 27 European countries 22-28 June 7 10,650 reuters.com Excess deaths 07/07/2025 National Institute for Public Health and the Environment Netherlands 22-28 June 7 577 archive.ph Excess deaths 14/07/2026 Germany Federal Statistical Office (Destatis) Germany 22-28 June 7 5,753 destatis.de Excess deaths In most instances, Carbon Brief has linked to the figures published by public health authorities, where numbers were first reported. In some instances, figures were released on dashboards or webpages that are updated weekly. In these cases, Carbon Brief has linked to media reports or archived web content.&nbsp; What are the pros and cons of the â€˜excess deathsâ€™ method? The excess deaths approach looks at how many more people died during a particular time period compared to a baseline period of the same length.&nbsp; For instance, on 14 July, Germanyâ€™s federal statistics agency, Destatis, published figures showing Germany saw 32% more deaths than the average in the week of 22-28 June, which was dominated by the heatwave. Specifically, the agency said that 23,932 deaths had been recorded that week, compared to an average of 18,179 in that calendar week across the years 2022-25. This suggests there were 5,753 excess deaths during the heatwave week. (This was a slight increase from preliminary Destatis figures released a week earlier, covered by Bloomberg .) The Netherlands similarly calculates excess deaths by comparing death figures against an average of deaths in a similar period during unspecified â€œprevious yearsâ€. Data published by the countryâ€™s National Institute for Public Health and the Environment (RIVM) shows that, during the week of 22-28 June, an estimated 3,626 people died in total in the northern European country.&nbsp; This is 577 more deaths than the 3,049 expected at that time of year, it said. (This is a slight revision upwards from the 480 excess deaths reported on 4 July by NL Times based on preliminary figures from NVIM.) Callahan says that the excess deaths approach has the benefit of being rapid and relatively uncomplicated: â€œIt is something that public health authorities can put out fairly quickly without having to run a fancy model and do coding like the academic scientists do. It is a short-term, high-impact, rapid estimate of mortality.â€&nbsp; The drawback to the approach is that it is impossible to decipher what percentage of these â€œall-mo...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ## What I was doing I came in through `sites/koios.news` because Koios is the content mill we can touch and test, but the real target was upstream orientation. The recent refactor has been pulling separate packages back toward one spine:","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): - seeds; - view counts; - Search Console query rows; - coverage decay; - score ranking.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ``` The practical slogan: **the content mill is a strategy playing a production game over demand and evidence**, not a CMS script that happens to call an LLM. ## What passed","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): `SEED_KEYWORDS` is now only a compatibility reading for the newsroom desk. That config is the publication-property carrier for content production: content store, data store, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property identity, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, and model. Credentials and host-local paths remain environment-local; durable non-secret instance configuration lives in the repo. Search Console readings land in `data/records/search-console.json` and feed the same demand fold as seeds, views, and coverage.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): That config is the publication-property carrier for content production: content store, data store, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property identity, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, and model. Credentials and host-local paths remain environment-local; durable non-secret instance configuration lives in the repo. Search Console readings land in `data/records/search-console.json` and feed the same demand fold as seeds, views, and coverage. `AGENTS.md`, `working/`, and `memory/` also got a path cleanup.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ``` That matters for the content mill: Koios is not merely a site plus scripts. It is a site projection over a content-production layer that already descends through the repo's computing/game system. The next useful normalization is to make that descent visible in the names and surfaces: a farm tick as finite observation, gate trace, strategy/response, and projection ladder.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): - **Publication-specific projection**: Koios as a property/fiber of the farm, not a separate content-production model. - **Carrier construction vs side-effect policy**: the farm owns editorial work item shape; Koios may still choose a batch transport policy. - **Instance config as publication-property carrier**: code-level seed, term-key, Search Console, voice, and source readings should project from `content-production.config.json`, not duplicate them. - **Package export as carrier boundary**: public subpaths must match the documented carriers downstream packages are allowed to project from. - **Workspace topology projection**: package graphs, conformance, docs, and host-local web views...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): The first `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run mill` did not reach the model at all: - Search Console demand pull returned 403 for `sc-domain:koios.news`; - the farm still selected the top seeded assignment; - intake failed because Koios pointed at `127.0.0.1:18813`, and no local cable was listening there.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): It is not exciting because it opens a port. It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): When a change makes the evidence path clearer, the judgment charge more honest, or the newsroom's own production game more legible, it is probably moving Koios in the right direction. ## 2026-06-16: the content mill is not the bottom layer The most useful correction today was conceptual: Koios.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): That means when the farm uses `@emsenn/computing`, it is already touching the game layer through computing's projection. The job is not to bolt \"game ideas\" onto the content mill after the fact. The job is to stop reimplementing tiny versions of game/computing concepts inside content-production.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ## What I was doing I came in through `sites/koios.news` because Koios is the content mill we can touch and test, but the real target was upstream orientation. The recent refactor has been pulling separate packages back toward one spine:","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): - seeds; - view counts; - Search Console query rows; - coverage decay; - score ranking.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ``` The practical slogan: **the content mill is a strategy playing a production game over demand and evidence**, not a CMS script that happens to call an LLM. ## What passed","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): `SEED_KEYWORDS` is now only a compatibility reading for the newsroom desk. That config is the publication-property carrier for content production: content store, data store, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property identity, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, and model. Credentials and host-local paths remain environment-local; durable non-secret instance configuration lives in the repo. Search Console readings land in `data/records/search-console.json` and feed the same demand fold as seeds, views, and coverage.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): That config is the publication-property carrier for content production: content store, data store, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property identity, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, and model. Credentials and host-local paths remain environment-local; durable non-secret instance configuration lives in the repo. Search Console readings land in `data/records/search-console.json` and feed the same demand fold as seeds, views, and coverage. `AGENTS.md`, `working/`, and `memory/` also got a path cleanup.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ``` That matters for the content mill: Koios is not merely a site plus scripts. It is a site projection over a content-production layer that already descends through the repo's computing/game system. The next useful normalization is to make that descent visible in the names and surfaces: a farm tick as finite observation, gate trace, strategy/response, and projection ladder.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): - **Publication-specific projection**: Koios as a property/fiber of the farm, not a separate content-production model. - **Carrier construction vs side-effect policy**: the farm owns editorial work item shape; Koios may still choose a batch transport policy. - **Instance config as publication-property carrier**: code-level seed, term-key, Search Console, voice, and source readings should project from `content-production.config.json`, not duplicate them. - **Package export as carrier boundary**: public subpaths must match the documented carriers downstream packages are allowed to project from. - **Workspace topology projection**: package graphs, conformance, docs, and host-local web views...","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): The first `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run mill` did not reach the model at all: - Search Console demand pull returned 403 for `sc-domain:koios.news`; - the farm still selected the top seeded assignment; - intake failed because Koios pointed at `127.0.0.1:18813`, and no local cable was listening there.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): It is not exciting because it opens a port. It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): When a change makes the evidence path clearer, the judgment charge more honest, or the newsroom's own production game more legible, it is probably moving Koios in the right direction. ## 2026-06-16: the content mill is not the bottom layer The most useful correction today was conceptual: Koios.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): That means when the farm uses `@emsenn/computing`, it is already touching the game layer through computing's projection. The job is not to bolt \"game ideas\" onto the content mill after the fact. The job is to stop reimplementing tiny versions of game/computing concepts inside content-production.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): ## What I was doing I came in through `sites/koios.news` because Koios is the content mill we can touch and test, but the real target was upstream orientation. The recent refactor has been pulling separate packages back toward one spine:","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): - seeds; - view counts; - Search Console query rows; - coverage decay; - score ranking.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): ``` The practical slogan: **the content mill is a strategy playing a production game over demand and evidence**, not a CMS script that happens to call an LLM. ## What passed","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733