fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): - **Publication-specific projection**: Koios as a property/fiber of the farm, not a separate content-production model. - **Carrier construction vs side-effect policy**: the farm owns editorial work item shape; Koios may still choose a batch transport policy. - **Instance config as publication-property carrier**: code-level seed, term-key, Search Console, voice, and source readings should project from `content-production.config.json`, not duplicate them. - **Package export as carrier boundary**: public subpaths must match the documented carriers downstream packages are allowed to project from. - **Workspace topology carrier**: package graphs, RDF, conformance, docs, and host-local web view...","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): The first `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run mill` did not reach the model at all: - Search Console demand pull returned 403 for `sc-domain:koios.news`; - the farm still selected the top seeded assignment; - intake failed because Koios pointed at `127.0.0.1:18813`, and no local cable was listening there.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): It is not exciting because it opens a port. It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): When a change makes the evidence path clearer, the judgment charge more honest, or the newsroom's own production game more legible, it is probably moving Koios in the right direction. ## 2026-06-16: the content mill is not the bottom layer The most useful correction today was conceptual: Koios.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): That means when the farm uses `@emsenn/computing`, it is already touching the game layer through computing's projection. The job is not to bolt \"game ideas\" onto the content mill after the fact. The job is to stop reimplementing tiny versions of game/computing concepts inside content-production.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts, sha256:92ed52f3999b1db4b87835c95a4966d7eb6ac2e85ea9f471deed212727aa2e0d): // researching/sourcing/source-cycle â€” one Koios-owned source refresh plus farm // tick. This is the operator-visible act that turns external feeds into local // wire evidence, then lets the content farm assemble context and draft. import { existsSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from \"node:fs\";","Prior research (sites/koios.news/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts, sha256:92ed52f3999b1db4b87835c95a4966d7eb6ac2e85ea9f471deed212727aa2e0d): constraints: Object.freeze([ \"This is a read-only operator backlog over farm run traces.\", \"Repair packets do not rewrite, publish, or silently normalize manuscripts.\", \"Intake sources remain research material; published citations remain CSL references in manuscript frontmatter and body links.\", \"A published candidate stays visible as historical friction but is not counted as open.\",","Prior research (sites/koios.news/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts, sha256:92ed52f3999b1db4b87835c95a4966d7eb6ac2e85ea9f471deed212727aa2e0d): record: \"draft-candidate\" as const, publication: \"manual-standards-gate\" as const, note: \"The source cycle can write draft candidates and run traces; Koios normalizes candidate citation metadata to CSL references and annotates drafts with same-site link guidance, but publishing remains a separate standards-gated newsroom action.\", }, });","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts, sha256:30b773ac4b106498aa17a37553fe6655bf5f022c6e65d9876ea1ead593e65e21): // researching/sourcing/source-cycle â€” one Koios-owned source refresh plus farm // tick. This is the operator-visible act that turns external feeds into local // wire evidence, then lets the content farm assemble context and draft. import { existsSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from \"node:fs\";","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts, sha256:30b773ac4b106498aa17a37553fe6655bf5f022c6e65d9876ea1ead593e65e21): const obstruction = runString(run, \"obstruction\"); if (obstruction === \"\") return null; const evidence = runString(run, \"evidence\") || `Source cycle obstruction: ${obstruction}`; return Object.freeze({ kind: \"source-alignment\" as const,","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts, sha256:30b773ac4b106498aa17a37553fe6655bf5f022c6e65d9876ea1ead593e65e21): constraints: Object.freeze([ \"This is a read-only operator backlog over farm run traces.\", \"Repair packets do not rewrite, publish, or silently normalize manuscripts.\", \"Intake sources remain research material; published citations remain CSL references in manuscript frontmatter and body links.\", \"A published candidate stays visible as historical friction but is not counted as open.\",","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts, sha256:30b773ac4b106498aa17a37553fe6655bf5f022c6e65d9876ea1ead593e65e21): record: \"draft-candidate\" as const, publication: \"manual-standards-gate\" as const, note: \"The source cycle can write draft candidates and run traces; Koios normalizes candidate citation metadata to CSL references and annotates drafts with same-site link guidance, but publishing remains a separate standards-gated newsroom action.\", }), });","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts, sha256:92ed52f3999b1db4b87835c95a4966d7eb6ac2e85ea9f471deed212727aa2e0d): // researching/sourcing/source-cycle â€” one Koios-owned source refresh plus farm // tick. This is the operator-visible act that turns external feeds into local // wire evidence, then lets the content farm assemble context and draft. import { existsSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from \"node:fs\";","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts, sha256:92ed52f3999b1db4b87835c95a4966d7eb6ac2e85ea9f471deed212727aa2e0d): constraints: Object.freeze([ \"This is a read-only operator backlog over farm run traces.\", \"Repair packets do not rewrite, publish, or silently normalize manuscripts.\", \"Intake sources remain research material; published citations remain CSL references in manuscript frontmatter and body links.\", \"A published candidate stays visible as historical friction but is not counted as open.\",","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts, sha256:92ed52f3999b1db4b87835c95a4966d7eb6ac2e85ea9f471deed212727aa2e0d): record: \"draft-candidate\" as const, publication: \"manual-standards-gate\" as const, note: \"The source cycle can write draft candidates and run traces; Koios normalizes candidate citation metadata to CSL references and annotates drafts with same-site link guidance, but publishing remains a separate standards-gated newsroom action.\", }, });","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:9d7ad9c99abd56d81c2ead53d8ea3c6bc2462982b367ae6411dc4dcdf3c17bd9): * repair-packets - the farm compost read as an operator repair backlog. * * A content farm run trace already records withheld gates and intake * obstructions. This module names the generic carrier that turns those records * into repair packets. Site packages may supply local candidate-state readers","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:9d7ad9c99abd56d81c2ead53d8ea3c6bc2462982b367ae6411dc4dcdf3c17bd9): * A content farm run trace already records withheld gates and intake * obstructions. This module names the generic carrier that turns those records * into repair packets. Site packages may supply local candidate-state readers * and constraint text, but the packet vocabulary belongs to producing-content. */","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:9d7ad9c99abd56d81c2ead53d8ea3c6bc2462982b367ae6411dc4dcdf3c17bd9): const obstruction = runString(run, \"obstruction\"); if (obstruction === \"\") return null; const evidence = runString(run, \"evidence\") || `Content farm obstruction: ${obstruction}`; return Object.freeze({ kind: \"source-alignment\" as const,","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:9d7ad9c99abd56d81c2ead53d8ea3c6bc2462982b367ae6411dc4dcdf3c17bd9): taskCount: tasks.length, tasks: [...tasks], }, \"content farm repair packet\"); }","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:9d7ad9c99abd56d81c2ead53d8ea3c6bc2462982b367ae6411dc4dcdf3c17bd9): constraints: [...(input.constraints ?? [ \"This is a read-only operator backlog over farm run traces.\", \"Repair packets do not rewrite, publish, or silently normalize manuscripts.\", \"Intake sources remain research material; published citations remain manuscript references.\", \"A published candidate stays visible as historical friction but is not counted as open.\",","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:9d7ad9c99abd56d81c2ead53d8ea3c6bc2462982b367ae6411dc4dcdf3c17bd9): \"A published candidate stays visible as historical friction but is not counted as open.\", ])], }, \"content farm repair packets reading\"); }","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:9d7ad9c99abd56d81c2ead53d8ea3c6bc2462982b367ae6411dc4dcdf3c17bd9): export function contentFarmRepairPacketTerm(input: ContentFarmRepairPacket): Tm { return carrierToTerm(jsonResource(contentFarmRepairPacketCarrier.assert(input), \"content farm repair packet\")); }","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:9d7ad9c99abd56d81c2ead53d8ea3c6bc2462982b367ae6411dc4dcdf3c17bd9): export function contentFarmRepairPacketsReadingTerm(input: ContentFarmRepairPacketsReading): Tm { return carrierToTerm(jsonResource(contentFarmRepairPacketsReadingCarrier.assert(input), \"content farm repair packets reading\")); }","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/lib/producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:676295442de55900f8fc91d33408f97a16e025323529b04e1b400666de17dbd2): * repair-packets - the farm compost read as an operator repair backlog. * * A content farm run trace already records withheld gates and intake * obstructions. This module names the generic carrier that turns those records * into repair packets. Site packages may supply local candidate-state readers","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/lib/producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:676295442de55900f8fc91d33408f97a16e025323529b04e1b400666de17dbd2): * A content farm run trace already records withheld gates and intake * obstructions. This module names the generic carrier that turns those records * into repair packets. Site packages may supply local candidate-state readers * and constraint text, but the packet vocabulary belongs to producing-content. */","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/lib/producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:676295442de55900f8fc91d33408f97a16e025323529b04e1b400666de17dbd2): const obstruction = runString(run, \"obstruction\"); if (obstruction === \"\") return null; const evidence = runString(run, \"evidence\") || `Content farm obstruction: ${obstruction}`; return Object.freeze({ kind: \"source-alignment\" as const,","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/lib/producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:676295442de55900f8fc91d33408f97a16e025323529b04e1b400666de17dbd2): taskCount: tasks.length, tasks: [...tasks], }, \"content farm repair packet\"); }","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/lib/producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:676295442de55900f8fc91d33408f97a16e025323529b04e1b400666de17dbd2): constraints: [...(input.constraints ?? [ \"This is a read-only operator backlog over farm run traces.\", \"Repair packets do not rewrite, publish, or silently normalize manuscripts.\", \"Intake sources remain research material; published citations remain manuscript references.\", \"A published candidate stays visible as historical friction but is not counted as open.\",","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/lib/producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:676295442de55900f8fc91d33408f97a16e025323529b04e1b400666de17dbd2): \"A published candidate stays visible as historical friction but is not counted as open.\", ])], }, \"content farm repair packets reading\"); }","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/lib/producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:676295442de55900f8fc91d33408f97a16e025323529b04e1b400666de17dbd2): export function contentFarmRepairPacketTerm(input: ContentFarmRepairPacket): Tm { return carrierToTerm(jsonResource(contentFarmRepairPacketCarrier.assert(input), \"content farm repair packet\")); }","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/lib/producing-content/src/content-farm/repair-packets.mts, sha256:676295442de55900f8fc91d33408f97a16e025323529b04e1b400666de17dbd2): export function contentFarmRepairPacketsReadingTerm(input: ContentFarmRepairPacketsReading): Tm { return carrierToTerm(jsonResource(contentFarmRepairPacketsReadingCarrier.assert(input), \"content farm repair packets reading\")); }"],"accountableAttention":{"rule":"make-the-click-preserve-the-warrant","headlineMustPreserve":["attribution","modality","scope","uncertainty","correction state"],"forbiddenStrengthenings":["source claim to newsroom fact","may/could/alleged to does/did/proves","single case to trend","correlation to causation","reader demand to evidence"],"correctionSurfacePolicy":"Any later correction to a cited claim must propagate to body copy, headline, social cards, feeds, JSON-LD, and syndicated packets."},"constraints":["This packet is research intake, not a publishable article.","Only current-provider claims are admitted as source-bound factual evidence.","Prior-corpus excerpts are contextual leads until separately sourced and admitted.","Publication still requires a licensed editorial state, exact-manuscript judgment, and explicit publishing action."]}

CURRENT BODY:
Recent reports from public health authorities and climate scientists estimate that Europe's June heatwave resulted in thousands of deaths, though the specific figures vary depending on the calculation method used. According to [Q&A: Europe's May and June heatwave deaths â€“ and how they were counted](https://www.carbonbrief.org/qa-europes-may-and-june-heatwave-deaths-and-how-they-were-counted/), France's national public health agency recorded 2,025 excess deaths during the week the heat peaked. In the same period, authorities in Germany and the Netherlands identified 5,753 and 533 excess deaths, respectively. An analysis from climate scientists in Carbon Brief found that France saw more than 2,700 heat-related deaths over 17 days in June. Separate research into the United Kingdom's heat events identified 2,700 deaths, with 42% attributed to human-caused climate change. These discrepancies in counting arise because researchers utilize different analytical frameworks to quantify mortality, each balancing specific advantages and drawbacks.

## The Evolution of Mortality Metrics

Economists and epidemiologists have been studying the relationship between heat and mortality for nearly a century. A pioneering study published in 1923 by geographer Ellsworth Huntington and economist Margaret Justin that looked at mortality data for New York City over 1882-88 found that deaths increased rapidly as temperatures rose above 17C. As global temperatures have risen in response to human-caused carbon emissions, scientists have increasingly sought to understand how warming could impact mortality. The study of mortality caused by specific heatwave events dates back a few decades, with a 1995 heatwave in Chicago among the earliest events to be studied in detail. Over the past decade, a growing number of studies have gone a step further, by estimating the number of deaths caused by a specific heatwave event and then attributing a percentage or number of those deaths to human-caused climate change. A 2016 study, for instance, found that human influence accounted for 506 of 735 summer deaths in Paris and 64 of 315 in London during a 2003 heatwave. Recent findings have quantified these links further, such as a 2018 Zurich study attributing 27% of heat-related deaths to global warming, and a 2021 British Columbia analysis assigning 11-15% of such deaths to the same cause.

## Methodological Divergence

Public health authorities currently rely on two primary, non-exclusive methods to estimate the human toll of heat. The "excess deaths" approach counts all fatalities during a heatwave that exceed a historical baseline for the same period. While this method allows authorities to produce rapid, high-impact estimatesâ€”such as the 32% increase in German deaths observed during the late June heatwaveâ€”it does not inherently isolate heat as the cause. Alternatively, statistical modelling uses long-term mortality data to determine the relationship between specific temperatures and death rates in a given region. This approach offers a more nuanced view but requires complex interdisciplinary expertise to run epidemiological models alongside counterfactual climate analyses. Death certificate coding, which explicitly records heatstroke as a cause of death, has fallen out of favor in many European jurisdictions because it fails to capture the full scope of heat-induced cardiovascular or respiratory failures.

## Implications for Public Health Data

The absence of a singular "ground truth" in mortality data mirrors the historical challenges of quantifying deaths from air pollution. While the scientific community has long accepted that particulate matter is toxic and lethal, the messaging surrounding heat-related mortality is more recent. Because high temperatures exacerbate chronic conditions like kidney injury, heart disease, and dementia, these deaths often appear on medical records as secondary or primary underlying causes rather than temperature-related incidents. As public health agencies continue to refine these methodologies, the reliance on models rather than certificates highlights a transition in how institutions monitor environmental risks. This shift indicates that the ability to track the human impact of a warming climate remains as much a project of statistical refinement as it is a process of public health reporting.
